WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_pgp_111'), 'During the next peak the api is slow but nothing errors. PgBouncer''s admin console shows (columns trimmed):

```text
pgbouncer=# SHOW POOLS;
 database | user | cl_active | cl_waiting | sv_active | sv_idle | maxwait | pool_mode
----------+------+-----------+------------+-----------+---------+---------+-------------
 rocket   | api  |        40 |         32 |        40 |       0 |       2 | transaction
```

Statement: "All 40 server connections of the pool are busy, so 32 clients wait in PgBouncer (the oldest for 2 seconds); PostgreSQL itself refused nobody."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. `sv_active 40` with `sv_idle 0` means the pool (default_pool_size 40) is fully used, and `cl_waiting 32` clients are queued inside PgBouncer; `maxwait` is how long the oldest has waited, in seconds. PostgreSQL sees only 40 connections, well under its limit. Waiting is the intended behaviour of a pool: the fix is shorter transactions or a carefully raised pool size, not more pods. The misconception is that waiting clients mean the database is rejecting connections.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_pgp_111') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
