```text
-- pg_stat_activity, 6 minutes in
 pid   | state  | wait_event_type | wait_event    | query
 41822 | active |                 |               | UPDATE orders SET phone_e164 = ...
 41907 | active | Lock            | transactionid | UPDATE orders SET status = ''delivered'' WHERE id = $1
 41911 | active | Lock            | transactionid | UPDATE orders SET status = ''picked_up'' WHERE id = $1
```

Statement: "Every row the big `UPDATE` has changed stays locked until its transaction commits, so status updates on those orders wait; batches of a few thousand rows, each committed on its own, release their rows within moments."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Row locks taken by `UPDATE` are held until the end of the transaction. One statement over 18 million rows is one transaction that lasts minutes, and every courier status update on an already-backfilled order waits for it (`wait_event = transactionid`). It also keeps old row versions from being vacuumed for its whole lifetime. Small batches commit quickly, so each lock is short and the backfill can pause or resume at any batch.', 3, 5
