WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_pgp_151'), 'The customer history query and its plan:

```sql
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
SELECT id, total_vnd, created_at FROM orders
WHERE customer_id = 42 AND created_at >= ''2026-10-01'' AND created_at < ''2026-11-01''
ORDER BY created_at DESC;
```

```text
 Index Scan Backward using orders_created_at_idx on orders  (actual time=0.03..212.40 rows=31 loops=1)
   Index Cond: ((created_at >= ''2026-10-01'') AND (created_at < ''2026-11-01''))
   Filter: (customer_id = 42)
   Rows Removed by Filter: 184211
   Buffers: shared hit=9120 read=2804
 Execution Time: 212.61 ms
```

Statement: "The index narrows the scan to October only, then 184,211 rows of other customers are read and thrown away; an index on `(customer_id, created_at)` would start at this customer''s October rows and return them already in order."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. `Index Cond` shows what the index narrowed: only the date range. `Filter` plus `Rows Removed by Filter` shows the work wasted on every other customer''s orders. With `(customer_id, created_at)` the equality column comes first, the range second: the scan jumps to customer 42, walks only October, and reads the rows in `created_at` order, so no sort is needed. With the columns the other way round the range would come first and the customer would still be a filter.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_pgp_151') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
